<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Wattasid Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1472 CE – 1554 CE · capital Fez</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The family of Marinid viziers who became sultans of Fez, ruling a shrunken Morocco squeezed between Portuguese enclaves and the rising Saadis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Wattas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanata-berbers">Zenata</a> kin of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marinid-sultanate">Marinids</a>, governed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> as regents and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> from 1420. After the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid</a> sultan was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1465, a sharif ruled the city until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-shaykh-al-wattasi">Muhammad al-Shaykh al-Wattasi</a>, who had just lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asilah">Arzila</a> to Portugal in 1471, captured Fez in 1472 and proclaimed himself sultan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wattasid authority rarely reached far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and the north. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hintata-emirate">Hintata emirate</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, local lords ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chefchaouen">Chefchaouen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a> seized much of the Atlantic coast. The dynasty relied on truces with Portugal and the prestige of Fez's scholars, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> brotherhoods led resistance to the Christians.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate</a>, leading holy war from the south, defeated the Wattasids and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1549. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abu-hassun">Ali Abu Hassun</a> briefly retook the city in 1554 with Ottoman help from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers</a>, but was killed the same year, ending the dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wattasid-sultanate.jpg" alt="Wattasid Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Vexilla Mundi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wattasid_dynasty_flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1472 CE – 1554 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fez</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad al-Shaykh, Muhammad al-Burtuqali, Ali Abu Hassun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Maliki)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wattasids, Banu Wattas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1513&amp;focus=wattasid-sultanate" aria-label="Wattasid Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4553 944 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wattasid Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4873 1200L4873 1200M4873 1200L4873 1200M4873 1200L4867 1162M4873 1200L4864 1148"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4873 1200h0M4873 1200h0M4867 1162h0M4864 1148h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4873 1200h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4873 1200h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Fez. <a href="/atlas?year=1513&amp;focus=wattasid-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
